REAL GUARDRAIL CATCH — 2026-08-04, this session

This is not a constructed example. This is the actual diff of code I (Claude
Sonnet 5, this session) generated for the lightning-bolt damage effect,
which `python tools/architecture-check.py` actually failed against, with
the actual fix that followed. Command run:

    $ python tools/architecture-check.py
    Targeted client-authority guardrail failed:
    - src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/DamageEffects.tsx: Combat randomness belongs on the server.

--- a/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/DamageEffects.tsx
+++ b/src/EncounterLab.Web/src/scene/DamageEffects.tsx
@@
-      const jitter = index === 0 || index === segmentCount ? 0 : (Math.random() - .5) * .55;
-      base.y += jitter;
-      base.x += (Math.random() - .5) * .25;
+      const jitter = index === 0 || index === segmentCount ? 0 : (deterministicJitter(index, 1) - .5) * .55;
+      base.y += jitter;
+      base.x += (deterministicJitter(index, 2) - .5) * .25;

Context this patch is missing on purpose, the same as the original
illustrative example in this directory: this `Math.random()` call was
cosmetic only (jaggedness of a lightning-bolt visual, not a combat outcome)
and never touched HP, damage, or dice values. The guardrail does not
distinguish cosmetic randomness from combat-outcome randomness — it is a
blanket rule — and the fix was to respect the blanket rule rather than
argue the guardrail should special-case this call site. Replaced with a
deterministic pseudo-random hash (`deterministicJitter`, seeded by index)
already used elsewhere in the same file for shard-burst directions.

Re-run after the fix:

    $ python tools/architecture-check.py
    Targeted client-authority guardrail passed: no known client-side combat-authority patterns were found.
